## 1.3 Describe Cloud Service Types

### IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service)
You rent the raw infrastructure — virtual machines, storage, networking — and manage everything above that (OS, runtime, apps, data) yourself.
- **Most control, most responsibility.**
- *Use case:* Lift-and-shift migration of an existing on-prem app to the cloud without redesigning it; when you need full control over the OS.
- Example: Azure Virtual Machines.

### PaaS (Platform as a Service)
Microsoft manages the underlying infrastructure AND the OS/runtime. You just deploy your application code and manage your data.
- **Balanced control** — you focus on building the app, not maintaining servers.
- *Use case:* Developers who want to build and deploy apps quickly without worrying about patching servers or configuring runtimes.
- Example: Azure App Service, Azure SQL Database.

### SaaS (Software as a Service)
A complete, ready-to-use application delivered over the internet. Microsoft manages literally everything except your data and user access.
- **Least control, least responsibility** — you just use the software.
- *Use case:* End users who need a working application immediately (email, office productivity, CRM).
- Example: Microsoft 365, Outlook on the web.

### Quick comparison
| Type | You manage | Provider manages | Example |
|---|---|---|---|
| IaaS | OS, runtime, apps, data | Physical infra, virtualization | Azure VMs |
| PaaS | Apps, data | OS, runtime, infra | Azure App Service |
| SaaS | Data, user access | Everything else | Microsoft 365 |

